<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial British East Africa Company</h1><p class="st-meta">1888 CE – 1895 CE · capital Mombasa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A chartered British trading company that governed much of Kenya and Uganda in the race for East Africa before handing it to the Crown.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The company was founded by the Scottish shipping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-mackinnon">William Mackinnon</a> and received a royal charter in 1888, following the Anglo-German agreement of 1886 that divided spheres of influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>. It leased the coastal strip from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar</a> and aimed to open trade routes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>With few resources, the company built forts along the caravan route inland and relied on treaties with local leaders. Its agent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a> reached the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda</a> in 1890, made a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwanga-ii">Mwanga II</a> and intervened in the conflict between Protestant and Catholic factions. The company also restored the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-toro">Kingdom of Toro</a>. Trade proved unprofitable and costs mounted.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The company could not afford its commitments, and the British government took over: the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uganda-protectorate">Uganda Protectorate</a> was declared in 1894 and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/east-africa-protectorate">East Africa Protectorate</a> in 1895, when the company surrendered its charter in return for compensation.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-british-east-africa-company.jpg" alt="Imperial British East Africa Company" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Imperial_British_East_Africa_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1888 CE – 1895 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mombasa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William Mackinnon, Frederick Lugard</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Swahili</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kenya">Kenya</a>, <a href="/countries/uganda">Uganda</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>IBEAC, IBEA Company territory</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1892&amp;focus=imperial-british-east-africa-company" aria-label="Imperial British East Africa Company on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5781 2329 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial British East Africa Company lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6101 2585L4997 651M6101 2585L6101 2585M6101 2585L5902 2432"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M6101 2585h0M5902 2432h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6101 2585h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6101 2585h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mombasa. <a href="/atlas?year=1892&amp;focus=imperial-british-east-africa-company">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
